  completeExplanation: 'Chapter 8 of Ganita Prakash transforms elementary drawing into rigorous geometric construction and spatial reasoning.\n\nThe chapter opens with artistic figures (faces, waves, eyes) and transitions from freehand approximation to mathematical precision. A circle is formalized as the locus of all points in a plane at a constant distance (radius r) from a fixed center P. The compass is introduced as a precision tool that guarantees equidistant loci and enables length transfer without relying on ruler numbers.\n\nSection 8.2 establishes formal definitions for rectangles (opposite sides equal, all angles 90 deg) and squares (all sides equal, all angles 90 deg). It introduces the crucial cyclic naming convention: vertices must be listed consecutively along the perimeter (e.g. ABCD, BCDA), while crossing internal diagonals (e.g. ABDC) is invalid. The section also proves Orientation Invariance: rotating a square or rectangle on a dot grid preserves all side lengths and angles, refuting the misconception that a tilted square becomes a "diamond".\n\nSection 8.3 guides students through step-by-step constructions of squares and rectangles, including subdividing rectangles into identical unit squares and building composite shapes like falling stepped squares.\n\nSections 8.4 and 8.5 explore internal relationships within rectangles. By varying line segment XY between opposite sides AD and BC, students observe that when AX = BY, segment XY is parallel to the base and equals AB. The shortest distance between opposite sides is the perpendicular width AB, and the longest distance is the diagonal BD. The properties of diagonals are thoroughly investigated: both rectangles and squares have diagonals of equal length that bisect each other. However, in a square, the diagonals are perpendicular and bisect the corner angles into 45-degree halves, whereas in a rectangle, diagonals divide corner right angles into unequal complementary angles (e.g. 60 deg and 30 deg). This leads to constructing rectangles given one side and diagonal length using compass arc intersections on perpendicular rays.\n\nFinally, Section 8.6 explores points equidistant from two fixed points. Drawing two circular arcs of equal radius from two centers yields intersection points that are equidistant from both. This principle is used to build the composite "House" figure (square base, equilateral triangle roof, curved roofline) and to construct a Rhombus-a 4-sided figure with 4 equal sides whose corner angles are not 90 degrees.',
